Building a price slider used to take a second request to find the cheapest and most expensive product. Ask for a number field as a facet and the response now includes `facetStats` with its range.

```json
{
  "facetStats": {
    "price": { "min": 19, "max": 349 }
  }
}
```

The range follows the query and the filters, so the slider always matches the results on screen. See [Faceted search](/docs/guides/faceted-search) for a full example.

## Added

- `facetStats` in search responses for number fields listed in `facets`.

## Improved

- Facet counts on indexes with more than a million documents are up to three times faster.

Facets are the lists of filters you see beside search results in a shop, each with a count: Audio (24), Lighting (9). ZeroIndex returns these counts in the same request as the results.

## Ask for facets

Any field in `filterableAttributes` can be a facet. Pass the ones you want to count.

<CodeGroup labels={["cURL", "JavaScript", "Python"]}>

```bash
curl https://api.zeroindex.test/v1/indexes/products/search \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ZEROINDEX_KEY" \
  -d '{ "q": "wireless", "facets": ["category", "brand"] }'
```

```js
const { hits, facetDistribution } = await client.index("products").search("wireless", {
  facets: ["category", "brand"],
});
```

```python
results = client.index("products").search("wireless", {"facets": ["category", "brand"]})
```

</CodeGroup>

The response counts how many matching documents have each value:

```json
{
  "facetDistribution": {
    "category": { "Audio": 24, "Accessories": 11, "Lighting": 3 },
    "brand": { "Vesterlund": 17, "Casa Bruna": 12, "Tarnelli": 9 }
  }
}
```

## Narrow down

When someone ticks a value, add it to the filter and search again. Values in the same facet are joined with `OR`, and different facets with `AND`.

```js
const selected = { category: ["Audio"], brand: ["Vesterlund", "Tarnelli"] };

const filter = Object.entries(selected)
  .map(([field, values]) => `${field} IN [${values.map((value) => `"${value}"`).join(", ")}]`)
  .join(" AND ");

await client.index("products").search("wireless", { filter, facets: ["category", "brand"] });
```

<Callout type="tip" title="Keep other options visible">
  If you count facets with the filter applied, every other brand drops to zero once one is ticked. Run a second search without the brand filter to keep their counts, and send both in one call with `multiSearch`.
</Callout>

## Price ranges

Number fields return their lowest and highest value in `facetStats`. Use them to set the ends of a price slider, then filter with `price 40 TO 120`.

```json
{
  "facetStats": {
    "price": { "min": 19, "max": 349 }
  }
}
```

ZeroIndex answers fast enough to search on every keystroke. This guide builds a search box in plain JavaScript that calls the API straight from the browser. The same steps apply in React, Vue or Svelte.

<Steps>
  <Step title="Create a search key">
    Never use an admin key in the browser. Create a search key in the dashboard, or a scoped key on your server if each user should only see their own documents.
  </Step>
  <Step title="Add the markup">

```html
<input type="search" placeholder="Search products" data-search />
<ul data-results></ul>
```

  </Step>
  <Step title="Search on input">
    Cancel the previous request whenever a new one starts, so a slow answer never overwrites a newer one.

```js
import { ZeroIndex } from "@zeroindex/client";

const client = new ZeroIndex("zi_search_7b1e04d6a3");
const index = client.index("products");
const input = document.querySelector("[data-search]");
const list = document.querySelector("[data-results]");
let controller;

input.addEventListener("input", async () => {
  controller?.abort();
  controller = new AbortController();

  const { hits } = await index.search(input.value, {
    limit: 8,
    attributesToHighlight: ["name"],
    signal: controller.signal,
  });

  list.innerHTML = hits.map((hit) => `<li>${hit._formatted.name}</li>`).join("");
});
```

  </Step>
  <Step title="Style the highlights">
    Matching words come back wrapped in `<mark>` tags. Style them to fit your design.

```css
[data-results] mark {
  background: none;
  font-weight: 600;
}
```

  </Step>
</Steps>

## Debouncing

You do not need to debounce: requests are cheap and usually answer in under 50 ms. If you search a very large index on slow connections, wait about 100 ms after the last keystroke before sending.

<Callout type="warning" title="Escape your data">
  `_formatted` only adds `<mark>` tags. If your documents can contain HTML written by users, escape it before you insert it into the page.
</Callout>

## Empty queries

An empty query returns the first documents by ranking. Show them as suggestions when the box gets focus, or clear the list instead.

Your database stays the source of truth, and ZeroIndex holds a copy of the fields you want to search. This guide uses Postgres and Node.js, but the pattern works with any database: one full import, then small updates as rows change.

<Steps>
  <Step title="Choose the fields">
    Only send what people search, filter, sort or see in results. Leaving out large text fields keeps the index small and fast.

```js
const toDocument = (row) => ({
  id: row.id,
  name: row.name,
  brand: row.brand,
  category: row.category,
  price: Number(row.price),
  in_stock: row.stock > 0,
  updated_at: Math.floor(row.updated_at.getTime() / 1000),
});
```

  </Step>
  <Step title="Import everything once">
    Read the table in pages and send each page as a batch.

```js
import pg from "pg";
import { ZeroIndex } from "@zeroindex/client";

const db = new pg.Pool();
const index = new ZeroIndex(process.env.ZEROINDEX_KEY).index("products");

let lastId = 0;
while (true) {
  const { rows } = await db.query(
    "select * from products where id > $1 order by id limit 1000",
    [lastId],
  );
  if (rows.length === 0) break;
  await index.addDocuments(rows.map(toDocument));
  lastId = rows.at(-1).id;
}
```

  </Step>
  <Step title="Send changes as they happen">
    Update the index wherever your app writes to the table. Send the document after the database transaction commits, so a rollback never leaves the index ahead of the database.

```js
export async function saveProduct(product) {
  const row = await db.query("update products set ... returning *", [...]);
  await index.updateDocuments([toDocument(row.rows[0])]);
}

export async function removeProduct(id) {
  await db.query("delete from products where id = $1", [id]);
  await index.deleteDocument(id);
}
```

  </Step>
  <Step title="Catch anything you missed">
    Run a small job every few minutes that sends rows changed since the last run. It covers writes made outside your app, like a migration or a manual fix.

```js
const { rows } = await db.query("select * from products where updated_at > $1", [lastRun]);
if (rows.length > 0) await index.updateDocuments(rows.map(toDocument));
```

  </Step>
</Steps>

## Rebuilding without downtime

To change your document shape or settings from scratch, import into a new index such as `products_new`, then swap it with the live one. Searches move over in one step, with no empty results in between.

```js
await client.swapIndexes([{ indexes: ["products", "products_new"] }]);
```

<Callout type="note">
  After the swap, `products_new` holds the old documents. Delete it once you are happy with the new index.
</Callout>

